---
import Content from "../Content.astro";
import SectionHead from "../SectionHead.astro";
import QuestionIndex from "./QuestionIndex.astro";
import QuestionHead from "./QuestionHead.astro";
import ProjectPubs from "../../layouts/ProjectPubs.astro";
import PubButton from "../buttons/PubButton.astro";
import Link from "../Link.astro";
import EmbeddingPipeline from "./software/EmbeddingPipeline.astro";
import MorphospacePlot from "./biocosmos/MorphospacePlot.tsx";
import PlannerPlot from "./biocosmos/PlannerPlot.tsx";
import MimicryPlot from "./biocosmos/MimicryPlot.tsx";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import lepiverseHome from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-home.jpg";
import lepiverseSpecies from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-species.jpg";
import lepiverseSimilar from "../../assets/img/biocosmos/lepiverse-similar-species.jpg";
import mimicry from "../../data/biocosmos/mimicry.json";
import stats from "../../data/biocosmos/morphospace-stats.json";

const morphoStats = [
  {
    value: stats.species.toLocaleString(),
    label: "species in the morphospace",
  },
  {
    value: stats.bothSides.toLocaleString(),
    label: "seen from both wing sides",
  },
  {
    value: `${stats.generaSignificant}/${stats.genera}`,
    label:
      "genera with integrated dorsal and ventral patterns (Mantel p < 0.05)",
  },
  {
    value: `r = ${stats.scopeMantelR}`,
    label: "dorso-ventral integration across all species",
  },
];

const random = mimicry.permutations.find((p) => p.null === "Random")!;
const mimicryStats = [
  {
    value: `${mimicry.pairs.filter((p) => p.recovered).length}/${mimicry.counts.testedPairs}`,
    label: "pairs mutually in the top 10 of 8,409 species on the dorsal side",
  },
  {
    value: random.observed.toFixed(3),
    label: "mean dorsal partner percentile (chance = 0.50)",
  },
  {
    value: "p ≤ 0.0001",
    label: "against random and congener nulls (10,000 permutations)",
  },
];

// From the lepiverse.com collection summary (October 2026).
const collection = [
  { value: "619,739", label: "specimen images" },
  { value: "9,342", label: "species" },
  { value: "196", label: "countries" },
  { value: "8", label: "validated families" },
];

// The research questions drive both the index cards at the top and the
// heading of each section, so the two stay in sync.
const questions = [
  {
    id: "ai-morphospace",
    question:
      "Are dorsal and ventral wing patterns integrated or decoupled across butterflies?",
    topic: "Dorso-ventral integration in the butterfly wing morphospace",
  },
  {
    id: "ai-mimicry",
    question: "Does visual similarity recover butterfly mimicry?",
    topic: "Testing image embeddings against known mimicry pairs",
  },
  {
    id: "llm-benchmark",
    question:
      "Which open-weight language model best turns plain questions into searches?",
    topic: "Choosing an open-weight LLM for natural-language search",
  },
  {
    id: "ai-biocosmos",
    question:
      "Can AI make large collections explorable for anyone with a browser?",
    topic: "Putting it all together: BioCosmos",
  },
];

const shotClasses =
  "group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 bg-forest-50 p-1.5 transition duration-500 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-lg dark:border-forest-100/10 dark:bg-forest-900/40";
const figureClasses =
  "rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 bg-white/70 p-3 shadow-sm dark:border-forest-100/10 dark:bg-forest-950/40 sm:p-5";
---

<QuestionIndex questions={questions} />

<SectionHead text="AI-assisted phenomics: studying complex traits at scale" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Coloration is a complex, multidimensional phenotype that is difficult to
    compare consistently across large numbers of samples. Its variation can
    reflect shared ancestry, convergence, and environmental selection. Image
    embeddings provide a scalable way to quantify this variation: a vision model
    converts each specimen photograph into a numerical representation. These
    embeddings place visually similar specimens near one another in trait space.
    This makes appearance measurable across hundreds of thousands of digitized
    specimens. I use these embeddings to identify visually similar species, map
    the morphospace of butterfly wing patterns, and test whether phenotypic
    similarity recovers known mimicry relationships. Combined with language
    models for natural-language search, this work shows how I use emerging AI
    tools to extract evolutionary information from natural history collections.
  </p>
  <EmbeddingPipeline />
</Content>

<QuestionHead questions={questions} id="ai-morphospace" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Dorsal and ventral wing patterns are largely integrated but not locked
    together. Across {stats.bothSides.toLocaleString()} species imaged on both
    surfaces, distances between species on the dorsal side track their distances
    on the ventral side (Mantel r = {stats.scopeMantelR}). Within genera, the
    two surfaces are significantly integrated in {stats.generaSignificant}
    of {stats.genera} genera (Mantel p &lt; 0.05); the rest diverge. This
    partial decoupling is consistent with the two surfaces facing different
    selection, such as signaling on the dorsal side and crypsis on the ventral
    side at rest. Skippers (Hesperiidae) and whites and sulphurs (Pieridae)
    separate clearly on both surfaces, whereas many lineages shift position
    between them.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-2">
    Each point is a species centroid in a principal-component space of image
    embeddings of digitized specimens. Switch surfaces, hover a point for its
    name, or pick a family to highlight it.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <MorphospacePlot client:visible />
</figure>
<dl class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 lg:grid-cols-4">
  {morphoStats.map((s) => (
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-3 dark:border-forest-100/10">
      <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
      <dd class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">
        {s.value}
      </dd>
      <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
        {s.label}
      </dd>
    </div>
  ))}
</dl>

<QuestionHead questions={questions} id="ai-mimicry" />
<Content>
  <p>
    Mimicry is a direct test of whether image embeddings capture what predators
    perceive. I compiled published Müllerian and Batesian butterfly mimicry
    pairs and, using dorsal wings, ranked how similar each partner is to the
    other among all species in the collection. Mimicry pairs rank far above
    chance, and above congeners. The misses are informative: in{" "}
    <i>Papilio polytes</i> and <i>P. glaucus</i> only some females mimic, and{" "}
    <i>Heliconius timareta</i> varies across its range. More work is needed to
    account for these variations. However, this initial work shows the potential
    of image embeddings to capture biologically meaningful visual similarity,
    and that this similarity can be used to study mimicry, species interactions,
    and phenotypic evolution. Use the interactive plots below to explore the
    results.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <MimicryPlot client:visible />
</figure>
<dl class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
  {mimicryStats.map((s) => (
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-3 dark:border-forest-100/10">
      <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
      <dd class="font-serif text-xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">
        {s.value}
      </dd>
      <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
        {s.label}
      </dd>
    </div>
  ))}
</dl>

<QuestionHead questions={questions} id="llm-benchmark" />
<Content>
  <p>
    I designed natural-language search for a large specimen collection. The
    system uses a language model to parse a question such as “orange butterflies
    with black stripes from Peru” into structured search calls (color, country,
    trait, or similarity). To choose the best model for production, I benchmark
    open-weight models on the same set of queries, comparing accuracy,
    consistency, latency, and token cost. Open-weight models keep the search
    reproducible and let any institution with limited resources, run it on its
    own server. It protects sensitive data, and allows for fine-tuning on
    domain-specific text. See the interactive plot below to explore the results
    of the benchmark.
  </p>
</Content>
<figure class={`not-prose my-6 ${figureClasses}`}>
  <PlannerPlot client:visible />
</figure>

<QuestionHead questions={questions} id="ai-biocosmos" />
<Content>
  <p>
    <Link text="BioCosmos" url="https://lepiverse.com" /> brings this work
    together in a low-cost web platform, deployed through{" "}
    <Link text="LepiVerse.com" url="https://lepiverse.com/" />, for more than
    600,000 digitized Lepidoptera specimen images. Image embeddings power
    visual-similarity search and interactive morphospace plots, an open-weight
    language model turns plain-language questions into searches, and taxonomic
    harmonization and geographic validation keep the underlying data clean. The
    result shows that emerging AI tools can make large natural history
    collections explorable for anyone with a browser. To see how the system is
    designed, explore the{" "}
    <a
      href="#biocosmos-architecture"
      class="text-mist-700 underline hover:text-mist-900 dark:text-mist-200 dark:hover:text-mist-100"
    >
      BioCosmos architecture
    </a>{" "}
    in the Software section.
  </p>
</Content>

<dl class="mx-auto mt-6 grid max-w-4xl grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-center sm:grid-cols-4">
  {collection.map((s) => (
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-forest-900/10 px-3 py-4 dark:border-forest-100/10">
      <dt class="sr-only">{s.label}</dt>
      <dd class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold text-moss-700 dark:text-moss-300">
        {s.value}
      </dd>
      <dd class="mt-1 text-xs text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
        {s.label}
      </dd>
    </div>
  ))}
</dl>

<div class="my-6 grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-2">
  <a
    href="https://lepiverse.com"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener"
    class={shotClasses}
  >
    <Image
      src={lepiverseHome}
      alt="LepiVerse home page with semantic, text, and image search beside a grid of butterfly specimens"
      width={900}
      loading="lazy"
      class="rounded-xl"
    />
    <p class="flex justify-between px-2 pt-1.5 text-xs">
      <span class="font-semibold text-forest-900 dark:text-forest-100">
        Semantic, text, and image search
      </span>
      <span class="font-mono text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-400">
        lepiverse.com
      </span>
    </p>
  </a>
  <a
    href="https://lepiverse.com/species/morpho_aega"
    target="_blank"
    rel="noopener"
    class={shotClasses}
  >
    <Image
      src={lepiverseSpecies}
      alt="LepiVerse species page for Morpho aega with specimen images, classification, and distribution map"
      width={900}
      loading="lazy"
      class="rounded-xl"
    />
    <p class="flex justify-between px-2 pt-1.5 text-xs">
      <span class="font-semibold text-forest-900 dark:text-forest-100">
        Enriched species page
      </span>
      <span class="font-mono text-forest-600 dark:text-forest-400">
        <i>Morpho aega</i>
      </span>
    </p>
  </a>
</div>

<figure class={shotClasses}>
  <Image
    src={lepiverseSimilar}
    alt="Visually similar species to Morpho aega. Dorsal matches are blue Morpho species; ventral matches include brown Morpho, Zeuxidia, Tanaecia, and Vindula species."
    width={1400}
    loading="lazy"
    class="w-full rounded-xl"
  />
  <figcaption class="px-2 pt-2 text-xs text-forest-800 dark:text-forest-200">
    <span class="font-semibold">Visually similar species.</span> Nearest
    neighbours of <i>Morpho aega</i> in embedding space. Dorsal matches are all
    blue
    <i>Morpho</i>, but the cryptic brown undersides also match <i>Zeuxidia</i>,
    <i>Tanaecia</i>, and <i>Vindula</i>, from different genera.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<Content>
  <p class="mt-6 mb-1 font-semibold text-moss-800 dark:text-moss-200">
    Publications:
  </p>
  <ProjectPubs author="Handika et al.">
    <PubButton
      author="Handika, H., Lürig, M. D., Nichtawitz, K. L., Dobon, O. M., Yarava, V. S., Sobayo, J. K., Shi, B., Elliott, M. J., Fortes, J. A. B., Porto, A"
      year="Submitted"
      title="BioCosmos: computer vision and large language models for large-scale image-based biodiversity data and trait exploration"
      doi="https://doi.org/10.32942/X2C11R"
      url="https://lepiverse.com"
      codeUrl="https://github.com/BioVisionLab/BioCosmos"
    />
  </ProjectPubs>
</Content>

<SectionHead text="Future directions" />
<Content>
  <p>
    I hope to extend this framework to Indo-Australian rodents, asking whether
    phenotypic divergence among isolated island and mountain populations
    parallels genomic divergence or reflects local environments, and, through
    collaborations, to frogs, reptiles, and birds.
  </p>
</Content>
